# Terminal and script colors

When generating scripts, terminal commands, or logging that use color:

1. ONLY use inline ANSI escape sequences (e.g. `\033[31m`) or standard library color wrappers.
2. ALWAYS terminate every colored string with the reset code (`\033[0m`) so the user's terminal theme is preserved.
3. Apply colors strictly to status tags/flags (e.g. `[ERROR]` in red, `[WARN]` in yellow, `[SUCCESS]` in green), not the whole line.
4. DO NOT generate commands that alter global terminal settings (Windows `color`, background fills, etc.).

Example:

```bash
printf '\033[31m[ERROR]\033[0m Failed to connect\n'
printf '\033[33m[WARN]\033[0m Retrying\n'
printf '\033[32m[SUCCESS]\033[0m Done\n'
```
